{"name":"Jumping Juniper","hex":"#887636","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"jumping","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Exuberantly active; in full swing."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["present participle and gerund of jump"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/jumping"},{"term":"juniper","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any shrub or tree of the genus Juniperus of the cypress family, which is characterized by pointed, needle-like leaves and aromatic berry-like cones.","One of a number of coniferous trees which resemble junipers."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/juniper"}],"entries":[{"title":"Jumping","blocks":[{"text":"Jumping or leaping is a form of locomotion or movement in which an organism or non-living mechanical system propels itself through the air along a ballistic trajectory. Jumping can be distinguished from running, galloping and other gaits where the entire body is temporarily airborne by the relatively long duration of the aerial phase and high angle of initial launch."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8f/Rehbock_auf_der_Ostplate_Spiekeroog_-_Nationalpark_nieders%C3%A4chsisches_Wattenmeer.jpg/330px-Rehbock_auf_der_Ostplate_Spiekeroog_-_Nationalpark_nieders%C3%A4chsisches_Wattenmeer.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jumping","wikidata":"Q1151752"},{"title":"Juniper","blocks":[{"text":"Junipers are coniferous trees and shrubs in the genus Juniperus of the cypress family Cupressaceae. Depending on the taxonomic authority, between 50 and 67 species of junipers are widely distributed throughout the Northern Hemisphere as far south as tropical Africa, as far north as the Arctic, and parts of Asia and Central America. The highest-known juniper forest occurs at an altitude of 4,900 metres (16,100 ft) in southeastern Tibet and the northern Himalayas, creating one of the highest tree lines on earth."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Juniperus_osteosperma_1.jpg/330px-Juniperus_osteosperma_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Juniper","wikidata":"Q25662"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Tursiops_truncatus_01.jpg/500px-Tursiops_truncatus_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Tursiops_truncatus_01.jpg/1280px-Tursiops_truncatus_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/10/Tursiops_truncatus_01.jpg/500px-Tursiops_truncatus_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Jumping bottlenose dolphin","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATursiops_truncatus_01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/SplitLeap.gif/250px-SplitLeap.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/ae/SplitLeap.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ae/SplitLeap.gif/250px-SplitLeap.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A split leap executed by an acro dancer. This is one of several types of leaps found in dance.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASplitLeap.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Frog_limbs.jpg/500px-Frog_limbs.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/Frog_limbs.jpg/500px-Frog_limbs.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbn